1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:07,224
Above the old Inca capital of Cusco, high in the Andes, three great walls zigzag across a hillside.

2
00:00:07,224 --> 00:00:10,983
Some of their stones weigh more than a hundred tonnes.

3
00:00:10,983 --> 00:00:16,899
And they fit together so tightly that you cannot slide a sheet of paper between them.

4
00:00:17,366 --> 00:00:18,077
No mortar.

5
00:00:18,077 --> 00:00:23,264
Curving seams, rounded corners, joints like the edges of a jigsaw puzzle.

6
00:00:23,264 --> 00:00:29,233
For many visitors the first reaction is simple: no human hands could have done this.

7
00:00:29,699 --> 00:00:31,445
And so the theories began.

8
00:00:31,445 --> 00:00:35,407
That the stone was melted, by heat or by some lost machine.

9
00:00:35,407 --> 00:00:39,033
That it was softened like clay and pressed into place.

10
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:43,733
That visitors from somewhere else left their marks on a Peruvian hill.

11
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:47,674
But the people who built this place kept records.

12
00:00:47,674 --> 00:00:49,306
Not on paper, on cords.

13
00:00:49,306 --> 00:00:54,766
The Inca recorded labour, tribute and stores on knotted strings called khipu.

14
00:00:55,233 --> 00:00:58,127
So today we keep this case the Inca way.

15
00:00:58,127 --> 00:01:00,225
Seven cords, seven questions.

16
00:01:00,225 --> 00:01:05,289
On each one we tie a knot for the evidence, and a knot for the theory.

17
00:01:05,289 --> 00:01:07,966
At the end, we read the summary cord.

18
00:01:12,433 --> 00:01:13,915
Cord one: the stone.

19
00:01:13,915 --> 00:01:22,733
Sacsayhuamán, in Quechua Saksaywaman, sits at about three thousand seven hundred metres, on the northern edge of Cusco.

20
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,029
Its most famous part is three terrace walls, laid out in a zigzag.

21
00:01:28,029 --> 00:01:31,175
The longest runs about four hundred metres.

22
00:01:31,175 --> 00:01:35,200
They hold more than six thousand cubic metres of stone.

23
00:01:35,666 --> 00:01:37,674
The giants are grey limestone.

24
00:01:37,674 --> 00:01:44,566
Estimates for the largest block run from about a hundred and twenty-eight tonnes to almost two hundred.

25
00:01:45,033 --> 00:01:47,934
Look closer and something else stands out.

26
00:01:47,934 --> 00:01:50,006
The stones are not rectangles.

27
00:01:50,006 --> 00:01:54,496
Each has its own outline, and every neighbour is cut to match it.

28
00:01:54,496 --> 00:01:57,466
Archaeologists call this polygonal masonry.

29
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:03,575
The walls also lean slightly inward, with rounded corners and interlocking joints.

30
00:02:03,575 --> 00:02:05,983
In an earthquake zone that matters.

31
00:02:05,983 --> 00:02:12,176
Cusco was shaken by major quakes in sixteen fifty, nineteen fifty and nineteen eighty-six.

32
00:02:12,176 --> 00:02:15,066
The great terrace walls came through them.

33
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:20,206
One more fact for this cord: the hill was not empty before the Inca.

34
00:02:20,206 --> 00:02:32,233
Pottery shows people here from around nine hundred CE, and in two thousand and eight archaeologists uncovered a temple from the earlier Killke culture at the edge of the site.

35
00:02:32,699 --> 00:02:37,491
So the stone is real, the scale is real, and the precision is real.

36
00:02:37,491 --> 00:02:38,707
Knot one is tied.

37
00:02:38,707 --> 00:02:40,066
Now for the claims.

38
00:02:40,533 --> 00:02:42,061
Cord two: the claims.

39
00:02:42,061 --> 00:02:50,066
In nineteen sixty-eight, Erich von Däniken's Chariots of the Gods brought Sacsayhuamán to millions of readers.

40
00:02:50,533 --> 00:02:58,817
He described a carved block near the fortress, the size of a four-storey house, with steps and ramps, lying upside down.

41
00:02:58,817 --> 00:03:05,099
He put its weight at twenty thousand tons, and asked what titanic forces had overturned it.

42
00:03:05,566 --> 00:03:07,018
Then he went further.

43
00:03:07,018 --> 00:03:17,112
Barely nine hundred yards away, he wrote, were rock vitrifications that ought only to be possible by melting stone at extremely high temperatures.

44
00:03:17,112 --> 00:03:20,500
The glacier explanation, he said, was ridiculous.

45
00:03:20,966 --> 00:03:22,954
Later writers built on that.

46
00:03:22,954 --> 00:03:37,366
The Peruvian Alfredo Gamarra, and the authors Jan Peter de Jong and Christopher Jordan, point to glassy, shiny patches on the stones as signs of heat, and argue the blocks were once soft enough to mould.

47
00:03:37,833 --> 00:03:44,210
A cousin of the idea says the stone was liquefied and cast, like concrete, into moulds.

48
00:03:44,210 --> 00:03:50,733
Another says a lost civilisation built the walls long before the Inca, who only moved in.

49
00:03:51,199 --> 00:03:54,449
And there is the oldest version of all: a plant.

50
00:03:54,449 --> 00:04:00,678
Somewhere in the Andes, the story goes, grows a herb whose juice turns rock as soft as clay.

51
00:04:00,678 --> 00:04:04,266
Rub it on the stone, and the blocks squeeze together.

52
00:04:04,733 --> 00:04:06,584
These are testable claims.

53
00:04:06,584 --> 00:04:10,215
Softened stone should squeeze deep into its joints.

54
00:04:10,215 --> 00:04:11,923
Cast stone needs moulds.

55
00:04:11,923 --> 00:04:14,699
And a magic plant should leave a trace.

56
00:04:15,166 --> 00:04:16,338
Knot two is tied.

57
00:04:16,338 --> 00:04:20,266
Now we go where every building really begins: the quarry.

58
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:22,258
Cord three: the quarry.

59
00:04:22,258 --> 00:04:25,840
The great limestone blocks did not have to travel far.

60
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:33,600
There are quarries on the hills right above Sacsayhuamán, and big loose blocks still lie scattered across the slopes.

61
00:04:34,066 --> 00:04:44,599
The harder dark andesite used in Cusco came from farther away, mainly from the Rumiqolqa quarry, about thirty kilometres to the south-east.

62
00:04:44,599 --> 00:04:47,100
Those were mostly smaller stones.

63
00:04:47,566 --> 00:04:54,826
In the nineteen seventies and eighties, the architect Jean-Pierre Protzen spent months in these quarries.

64
00:04:54,826 --> 00:05:02,155
At Rumiqolqa he found dozens of round river cobbles lying in the debris, stones that did not belong there.

65
00:05:02,155 --> 00:05:03,399
They were hammers.

66
00:05:03,866 --> 00:05:07,666
The Spanish chroniclers had already described them.

67
00:05:07,666 --> 00:05:15,639
Garcilaso de la Vega called them hihuana, black stones that dress the rock by pounding rather than cutting.

68
00:05:15,639 --> 00:05:20,333
Bernabé Cobo wrote of hard, black cobblestones from the rivers.

69
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:22,495
Protzen tried it himself.

70
00:05:22,495 --> 00:05:34,433
Striking at a low angle, about fifteen to twenty degrees, and letting the hammer turn on impact, he could knock off flakes and shape a face far faster than most people imagine.

71
00:05:34,899 --> 00:05:37,735
And here is the first clue to the shine.

72
00:05:37,735 --> 00:05:45,959
When a hammerstone strikes limestone, the heat of the impact partly changes the rock and leaves a tiny whitish spot.

73
00:05:45,959 --> 00:05:50,000
The faces at Sacsayhuamán are covered in these pockmarks.

74
00:05:50,466 --> 00:05:56,824
And at the foot of the walls, excavators have found layers of small angular limestone chips.

75
00:05:56,824 --> 00:05:58,966
The waste of all that pounding.

76
00:05:59,433 --> 00:06:02,332
Melted stone does not carry hammer scars.

77
00:06:02,332 --> 00:06:04,737
These blocks have them everywhere.

78
00:06:04,737 --> 00:06:07,000
Knot three: pounded, not poured.

79
00:06:07,466 --> 00:06:08,991
Cord four: the haul.

80
00:06:08,991 --> 00:06:11,811
Here the chroniclers give us numbers.

81
00:06:11,811 --> 00:06:16,766
Pedro Cieza de León visited the site twice, around fifteen fifty.

82
00:06:17,233 --> 00:06:25,519
He was told the Inca ordered the provinces to send twenty thousand men, working in rotation so no one stayed too long.

83
00:06:25,519 --> 00:06:27,696
Four thousand quarried and cut.

84
00:06:27,696 --> 00:06:33,033
Six thousand hauled the stones with great cables of leather and plant fibre.

85
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:35,294
The rest dug foundations.

86
00:06:35,294 --> 00:06:37,160
Each lineage camped apart.

87
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:44,266
Overseers walked among them, with master builders who were, in his words, great masters of the art.

88
00:06:44,733 --> 00:06:48,623
This was the mit'a, labour owed to the state in turns.

89
00:06:48,623 --> 00:06:55,036
And the Inca counted it on khipus: how many workers each province owed, and for how long.

90
00:06:55,036 --> 00:06:58,566
The cords were the real machine behind the walls.

91
00:06:59,033 --> 00:07:00,673
Their ropes were strong.

92
00:07:00,673 --> 00:07:12,699
Each year a Quechua community still rebuilds the Q'eswachaka bridge from hand-twisted grass rope, and tests found a two-inch grass rope can hold more than five thousand pounds.

93
00:07:13,166 --> 00:07:16,750
Cieza also saw a huge block abandoned on the way.

94
00:07:16,750 --> 00:07:22,381
The workers told him the stone got tired, and they could not move it further.

95
00:07:22,381 --> 00:07:30,500
Tired stones like it still lie on the road to Ollantaytambo, resting on prepared beds of soil, gravel and clay.

96
00:07:30,966 --> 00:07:34,754
In nineteen ninety-six, a documentary crew tested it.

97
00:07:34,754 --> 00:07:43,545
About two hundred and fifty villagers from Ollantaytambo pulled a block of roughly fifteen tonnes up an incline with ropes.

98
00:07:43,545 --> 00:07:46,333
Once it started moving, it kept moving.

99
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:49,268
That is not a hundred-tonne block.

100
00:07:49,268 --> 00:07:55,874
But it shows the method grows with people, rope, ramps and time, and the Inca had all four.

101
00:07:55,874 --> 00:07:56,599
Knot four.

102
00:07:57,066 --> 00:07:58,375
Cord five: the fit.

103
00:07:58,375 --> 00:08:00,647
This is the heart of the mystery.

104
00:08:00,647 --> 00:08:04,366
How do you make two irregular stones meet with no gap?

105
00:08:04,833 --> 00:08:06,723
The chroniclers were blunt.

106
00:08:06,723 --> 00:08:13,093
Cobo wrote that to fit the stones they had to set them in place and remove them many times.

107
00:08:13,093 --> 00:08:18,833
José de Acosta said the fit had to be tried again and again, with great suffering.

108
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:20,719
Protzen reproduced it.

109
00:08:20,719 --> 00:08:26,977
He set a stone on its neighbour, lifted it off, and looked where the stone dust had been crushed.

110
00:08:26,977 --> 00:08:28,719
Those were the high points.

111
00:08:28,719 --> 00:08:31,106
He pounded them down and tried again.

112
00:08:31,106 --> 00:08:35,299
Within a few hours, using only hammerstones, he had a tight seam.

113
00:08:35,766 --> 00:08:37,879
There may have been a shortcut.

114
00:08:37,879 --> 00:08:49,466
The architect Vince Lee proposed that Inca masons scribed the outline of one stone onto its neighbour, the way log builders do, so the giants only had to be lowered once.

115
00:08:49,933 --> 00:08:51,435
And look at the lumps.

116
00:08:51,435 --> 00:08:58,265
Many Inca blocks carry small knobs near their lower edges, often knocked off once the stone was set.

117
00:08:58,265 --> 00:09:03,933
Bosses like these gave levers something to push against while a stone was adjusted.

118
00:09:04,399 --> 00:09:11,958
At Sacsayhuamán, the archaeologist John Rowe noticed that many stones have notches at the base instead.

119
00:09:11,958 --> 00:09:14,966
No doubt, he wrote, for the same purpose.

120
00:09:15,433 --> 00:09:17,855
Something else gives the game away.

121
00:09:17,855 --> 00:09:21,109
The perfect seam is mostly on the face you see.

122
00:09:21,109 --> 00:09:29,899
Behind it the joints open into wedge-shaped gaps, packed with sticky red clay the Spanish recorded by its Quechua name, llanca.

123
00:09:30,366 --> 00:09:36,032
If the stone had been softened and squeezed, it would squeeze all the way through.

124
00:09:36,032 --> 00:09:36,861
It does not.

125
00:09:36,861 --> 00:09:39,624
The precision stops where the eye stops.

126
00:09:39,624 --> 00:09:41,766
Knot five: fitted, not moulded.

127
00:09:42,233 --> 00:09:43,651
Cord six: the shine.

128
00:09:43,651 --> 00:09:45,566
Now for the vitrified rock.

129
00:09:46,033 --> 00:09:58,134
The polished rock about nine hundred yards from the walls is almost certainly the Rodadero, a hill of long glassy grooves where children have slid for centuries.

130
00:09:58,134 --> 00:10:01,366
Garcilaso de la Vega slid down it as a boy.

131
00:10:01,833 --> 00:10:10,335
In nineteen fourteen, the Yale geologist Herbert Gregory published a paper on it in the American Journal of Science.

132
00:10:10,335 --> 00:10:14,366
His title says it all: a fault plane of unusual aspect.

133
00:10:14,833 --> 00:10:21,806
Blocks of rock ground past each other along a nearly flat fault, polishing and grooving the surface.

134
00:10:21,806 --> 00:10:28,013
Glaciers are unlikely, since the nearest sign of glaciation is hundreds of metres higher.

135
00:10:28,013 --> 00:10:31,500
And it is not lava: the hill is not volcanic rock.

136
00:10:31,966 --> 00:10:36,334
So von Däniken was right that a glacier was a poor explanation.

137
00:10:36,334 --> 00:10:38,414
But the answer was never heat.

138
00:10:38,414 --> 00:10:40,633
It was the earth itself, moving.

139
00:10:41,100 --> 00:10:44,998
On the walls, the glassy patches have another source.

140
00:10:44,998 --> 00:10:52,133
Limestone dissolves in rainwater and redeposits as calcite, the same mineral as cave stalactites.

141
00:10:52,133 --> 00:10:58,533
It forms shiny crusts, often reddish with iron, and glows blue under ultraviolet light.

142
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:00,279
Here is the test.

143
00:11:00,279 --> 00:11:07,127
Inca walls of granite and andesite, at sites like Ollantaytambo, were dressed the same way.

144
00:11:07,127 --> 00:11:08,557
They have no glaze.

145
00:11:08,557 --> 00:11:12,621
A melting technology would not care what the rock was.

146
00:11:12,621 --> 00:11:13,599
Calcite does.

147
00:11:14,066 --> 00:11:15,066
And the plant?

148
00:11:15,066 --> 00:11:25,418
Its story goes back to sixteen thirty-eight, to a woodpecker said to use a herb that dissolved iron, which prisoners used to escape their chains.

149
00:11:25,418 --> 00:11:28,345
Similar tales exist in old European lore.

150
00:11:28,345 --> 00:11:31,986
No one has ever shown such a plant softening stone.

151
00:11:31,986 --> 00:11:34,200
Knot six: the shine is geology.

152
00:11:34,666 --> 00:11:36,353
Cord seven: the witnesses.

153
00:11:36,353 --> 00:11:39,533
Who did the people of Cusco say built this place?

154
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:41,811
They said the Inca did.

155
00:11:41,811 --> 00:11:52,522
The chroniclers recorded that work began under Pachacuti, and continued under his son Tupac Inca Yupanqui and his grandson Huayna Capac.

156
00:11:52,522 --> 00:11:56,066
Garcilaso said it took more than fifty years.

157
00:11:56,533 --> 00:11:58,038
That is not deep time.

158
00:11:58,038 --> 00:12:04,333
Huayna Capac died around fifteen twenty-four, less than a decade before the Spanish arrived.

159
00:12:04,333 --> 00:12:08,233
People who told this story could have known the builders.

160
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:16,832
Pedro Pizarro saw the fortress intact, its stones so large it seemed impossible human hands had set them.

161
00:12:16,832 --> 00:12:24,733
Cieza later watched Andean masons build Spanish houses with the same skill, using no tools but stones.

162
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:31,216
In fifteen thirty-six, Manco Inca rose against the Spanish and besieged Cusco.

163
00:12:31,216 --> 00:12:33,839
Sacsayhuamán became a battlefield.

164
00:12:33,839 --> 00:12:43,866
Juan Pizarro was fatally wounded there, and tradition says an Inca soldier named Cahuide leapt from a tower rather than surrender.

165
00:12:44,333 --> 00:12:46,314
Then the dismantling began.

166
00:12:46,314 --> 00:12:52,990
Garcilaso wrote that hardly a Spanish house in Cusco was not built from the fortress stone.

167
00:12:52,990 --> 00:12:56,585
What remains are the blocks too big to carry off.

168
00:12:56,585 --> 00:12:59,299
That is why only the giants are left.

169
00:12:59,766 --> 00:13:12,786
Even the rough upper courses that some point to as proof of an older, lost civilisation are partly modern: erosion walls added for visitors, missing in early twentieth-century photographs.

170
00:13:12,786 --> 00:13:15,833
Knot seven: the witnesses name the builders.

171
00:13:16,299 --> 00:13:18,147
Seven cords, seven knots.

172
00:13:18,147 --> 00:13:19,700
Now the summary cord.

173
00:13:20,166 --> 00:13:23,209
Hammerstone scars on the blocks: evidence.

174
00:13:23,209 --> 00:13:26,759
Fitting by trial and error, reproduced: evidence.

175
00:13:26,759 --> 00:13:31,613
Chroniclers naming the Inca, and twenty thousand workers: evidence.

176
00:13:31,613 --> 00:13:36,033
The glassy shine, from calcite and a fault polish: explained.

177
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:40,080
Alien tools, or heat-melted stone: no evidence.

178
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:42,823
A plant that softens rock: folklore.

179
00:13:42,823 --> 00:13:48,233
The exact way the largest blocks were lifted and fitted: still debated.

180
00:13:48,700 --> 00:13:49,543
The verdict.

181
00:13:49,543 --> 00:13:53,547
Sacsayhuamán was not melted, poured or beamed into place.

182
00:13:53,547 --> 00:14:04,366
Every line of evidence, from the stone dust to the chronicles, points to Inca masonry: pounding, testing and fitting, by thousands of people over decades.

183
00:14:04,833 --> 00:14:13,416
What stays open is the hardest engineering question: exactly how a hundred-tonne block was raised, tested and lowered again.

184
00:14:13,416 --> 00:14:18,399
That is a question for archaeologists with measurements, not for aliens.

185
00:14:18,866 --> 00:14:22,153
And these walls are not only a ruin of the past.

186
00:14:22,153 --> 00:14:34,066
Every twenty-fourth of June since nineteen forty-four, Inti Raymi, the festival of the Sun, is performed on the field in front of them, by people whose ancestors raised them.

187
00:14:34,533 --> 00:14:36,057
That's the summary cord.

188
00:14:36,057 --> 00:14:37,963
Which knot surprised you most?

189
00:14:37,963 --> 00:14:43,300
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
